<http://purl.org/nanopub/temp/np1/assertion> {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Proposed anatomical blueprint for \"a small freshwater animal that regenerates its limbs\": 8 organ systems, 38 organs, 92 tissues and 144 cell types that Uberon and the Cell Ontology record for Metazoa." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "The description \"a small freshwater animal that regenerates its limbs\" was resolved to Metazoa (NCBITaxon:33208), most specific of the taxon words found (subclass of the others). From that taxon the tool proposes a body > organ system > organ > tissue > cell hierarchy of existing ontology terms: 8 organ systems, 38 organs, 92 tissues and 144 cell types, distinct terms counted once each. A tissue or cell type that is part of several organs is shown under each, which is why the file lists 129 tissue and 205 cell placements. Structures read from the description and kept regardless of ranking: \"limbs\" as limb. Every content word of the description was recognised. Of the 39 organs, 0 carry a taxon basis stated by the ontology for this taxon or an ancestor, 35 are stated only for all animals, and 4 are merely not excluded. The per-parent limits cut a further 96 organs, 508 tissues and 559 cell types, each listed in the cited file with the query that found it; no structure named in the description was cut. The genome sketch is not a genome and is not part of this proposal: the Cell Ontology records marker gene products for 0 of the 144 cell types and nothing for the other 144. What was assembled is an anatomy, not an organism. This is a proposal assembled from ontology statements: it is not an observation of any organism, and no specimen, image or measurement enters it." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "ontology-derived design proposal" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Taxon Metazoa (NCBITaxon:33208), verified live as the most specific of the taxon words found. Selection: organ systems are subclasses of \"anatomical system\" in Uberon's core subsets, most general kept; organs are parts of a system in the major_organ, organ_slim, efo_slim or vertebrate_core subsets, ranked major organs first then by the number of cell types the Cell Ontology records for them, at most 6 per system; at most 6 tissues and 8 cell types per organ. Taxon filter: a structure is dropped when Uberon or the Cell Ontology states never_in_taxon on the term, a superclass, or a whole it is part of, for a taxon containing this one, or states in_taxon for a taxon unrelated to this one; for non-vertebrates the vertebrate_core subset is dropped as well. Absence of a constraint is not presence, which is why every organ carries its taxon evidence. Retrieved from Ubergraph on 2026-10-31T06:47:03.697Z; the cache pins the bytes. Unknown stays unknown: nothing was filled in where the ontologies are silent." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasEvidence> <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs-queries> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "Take a curated anatomical atlas for Metazoa that was not used here, and count two things: structures the atlas lists at organ level that this blueprint omits, and organs this blueprint proposes that the atlas does not record for the taxon. A blueprint whose \"stated\" organs disagree with the atlas would falsify the selection rule; disagreement confined to the \"not excluded\" organs would instead show the ontology's taxon constraints are too sparse, which is the limit already recorded here." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Does not establish: that an individual of Metazoa has these structures, that the list is complete, or that any part of it was measured. Uberon's taxon constraints are sparse, so organs marked \"not excluded\" may be absent in the organism; tissues and cell types are ontology part_of statements, not counts from a specimen. Nothing here is a genome, and no function is predicted." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/main/organism-design/examples/a-small-freshwater-animal-that-regenerates-its-limbs.json> .
}
